WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.752 --> 00:00:17.462
My name is Othon le Quarrelier, and I come from a family of stonemasons living in the town

2
00:00:17.562 --> 00:00:18.442
of Rouen, France.

3
00:00:19.363 --> 00:00:21.384
Of course I am a stonemason too.

4
00:00:22.025 --> 00:00:28.129
I wouldn't do anything else because over the centuries stonemasons have built marvelous structures in every corner of

5
00:00:28.149 --> 00:00:31.832
the world, from the Egyptian pyramids to the Parthenon in Greece.

6
00:00:32.948 --> 00:00:38.131
We stonemasons have always been in demand for our great skills, and the work we do, cutting and

7
00:00:38.191 --> 00:00:40.232
shaping stone, hasn't changed much.

8
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:49.898
But at this time, in 1220, stonemasons are building a greater variety of structures—bridges, cathedrals, and castles—than ever

9
00:00:49.958 --> 00:00:55.521
before. My father was a master stonemason, but he did not teach me the trade.

10
00:00:56.121 --> 00:01:00.444
He paid another master mason nearby to take me on as an apprentice when I was 11 years

11
00:01:00.584 --> 00:01:04.418
old. I lived with this master mason for seven years to learn the craft.

12
00:01:04.938 --> 00:01:09.742
I was taught how to select stone from the quarry, a deep excavation pit outside of town that

13
00:01:09.782 --> 00:01:11.243
contains the material we use.

14
00:01:12.063 --> 00:01:18.067
Then I learned how to use the tools of masonry: hammers, chisels, the compass, and the set square.

15
00:01:18.768 --> 00:01:23.131
The set square helps me make sure the walls I build are straight and true, so that my

16
00:01:23.191 --> 00:01:24.972
corners form ninety-degree angles.

17
00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:29.895
I learned how to use my hammer and chisel to turn a rough block of stone into a

18
00:01:29.975 --> 00:01:31.596
carved and polished building block.

19
00:01:32.356 --> 00:01:36.819
My master showed me how to work the stone carefully and patiently, and how to place the finished

20
00:01:36.859 --> 00:01:39.381
stone into position in a wall and set it in mortar.

21
00:01:40.262 --> 00:01:45.645
Eventually I left my teacher and began the life of a journeyman, someone who has finished his apprenticeship

22
00:01:45.965 --> 00:01:47.486
but is not yet a master mason.

23
00:01:48.147 --> 00:01:51.729
Journeyman masons often travel from place to place, looking for work.

24
00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:57.409
Whenever I came to a new town, I'd visit the local guildhall, a place where a stonemason can

25
00:01:57.469 --> 00:02:00.210
find a job, a place to sleep, and good food.

26
00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:06.653
Master masons at the guildhall test the journeymen, and if they're skilled and pass the test, the master

27
00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:07.553
masons hire them.

28
00:02:08.193 --> 00:02:12.835
Once a journeyman gets a job, he chisels his personal mark on the stone he carves so the

29
00:02:12.875 --> 00:02:14.736
master mason can evaluate his work.

30
00:02:16.357 --> 00:02:22.019
Some of the buildings that stonemasons create are so tall and beautiful that many people believe our skills

31
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:22.900
must be magical.

32
00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.582
But the secret to our success is geometry, which we learn early in our apprenticeship.

33
00:02:29.102 --> 00:02:35.605
Geometry is a branch of mathematics that involves the relationships between lines and angles, and the size, shape,

34
00:02:35.985 --> 00:02:37.686
and position of objects within space.

35
00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:40.227
Geometry makes masonry possible.

36
00:02:41.408 --> 00:02:46.190
After working as a journeyman for a number of years, I acquired the skills to become a master

37
00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.412
mason, and now I direct the masonry work at the cathedral in Chartres.

38
00:02:51.344 --> 00:02:57.627
Twenty-six years ago, in 1194, most of the building was destroyed when a lightning strike caused a huge

39
00:02:57.687 --> 00:03:00.288
fire, which consumed a lot of the town as well.

40
00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:04.750
People were very upset since the town was a popular pilgrimage destination.

41
00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:09.672
So the local bishop decided to rebuild the cathedral and make it much grander.

42
00:03:10.232 --> 00:03:12.773
It's taken a long time, but we're almost finished.

43
00:03:13.873 --> 00:03:20.772
Since I am responsible for the entire project, I hire the journeymen masons, carpenters, glassmakers, and all the

44
00:03:20.812 --> 00:03:22.033
other laborers necessary.

45
00:03:22.774 --> 00:03:26.977
Over two hundred people work for me, and I live in a temporary house right next to the

46
00:03:27.017 --> 00:03:30.259
construction site so I'm always on hand to supervise the work.

47
00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:37.224
My stonemasons are divided into two groups: one group works at the quarry five miles away, cutting and

48
00:03:37.284 --> 00:03:42.827
shaping the rocks, and the other group polishes the stone blocks on-site and sets them in place with

49
00:03:42.887 --> 00:03:49.192
mortar. To cut the rocks, a mason uses a number of tools, including axes, chisels, and hammers.

50
00:03:49.964 --> 00:03:53.867
The ax is used to hack at the rock to remove large chunks of unwanted stone.

51
00:03:54.808 --> 00:03:59.672
To make the rock even smaller, a mason uses a chisel to chip off smaller pieces of rock.

52
00:04:00.332 --> 00:04:05.236
By doing this at the quarry, we greatly reduce the rock's weight, which makes it easier to transport

53
00:04:05.296 --> 00:04:06.077
to the building site.

54
00:04:06.838 --> 00:04:11.882
The previous cathedral was small and had very few windows, so it was dark and gloomy inside.

55
00:04:12.762 --> 00:04:18.207
The new church has tall towers that seem to climb up to heaven and huge stained-glass windows that

56
00:04:18.267 --> 00:04:19.528
fill the cathedral with light.

57
00:04:20.384 --> 00:04:25.527
To achieve these breathtaking effects, I used full-scale drawings on plaster on the site's floor.

58
00:04:26.248 --> 00:04:30.851
They helped me visualize the project and calculate the amounts of materials and the number of men I

59
00:04:30.891 --> 00:04:31.251
would need.

60
00:04:32.152 --> 00:04:37.175
For this cathedral we worked from the early spring through the warm months, which allowed the mortar holding

61
00:04:37.195 --> 00:04:38.876
the stones in place to dry thoroughly.

62
00:04:39.836 --> 00:04:45.220
During the winter construction stopped, and the masons carved the statues on the west wall of the cathedral.

63
00:04:46.236 --> 00:04:50.777
Notice how their robes make the vertical lines of the soaring structure seem even taller.

64
00:04:51.498 --> 00:04:58.199
The masons also carved other smaller statues—such as gargoyles, which direct rainwater away from the building so it

65
00:04:58.219 --> 00:04:59.600
does not damage the foundation.

66
00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:03.421
Masons must pay very close attention to small details.

67
00:05:04.161 --> 00:05:09.642
As the cathedral began to rise, my masons had to work at great heights—very dangerous work.

68
00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:14.884
My former teacher was badly hurt when he fell during the construction of the cathedral in Reims.

69
00:05:16.448 --> 00:05:20.050
Getting building materials to these heights was difficult, but we managed.

70
00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:25.472
We accomplished this by leaving openings, called putlog holes, in the stone at regular intervals.

71
00:05:26.152 --> 00:05:30.994
We used the holes to hold logs on which we placed wood planks to create platforms that can

72
00:05:31.014 --> 00:05:31.974
be reached by a ladder.

73
00:05:32.555 --> 00:05:36.876
Then we used special cranes to hoist the stone up to our workmen on the platforms.

74
00:05:37.577 --> 00:05:42.819
When the building is finished, the logs and platforms will be removed and the putlog holes filled in

75
00:05:42.839 --> 00:05:43.319
with mortar.

76
00:05:43.899 --> 00:05:46.797
It can be dangerous work, but I love working with stone.

77
00:05:47.317 --> 00:05:49.839
And I'm proud that this cathedral will live on after me.

78
00:05:50.319 --> 00:05:56.704
It will proclaim to future generations that whenever the highest quality of craftsmanship is needed, stonemasons will be

79
00:05:56.744 --> 00:05:56.944
there.
